# Line endings are decided here, in the repo, instead of by whatever core.autocrlf
# happens to be set to on the machine doing the checkout. Git for Windows sets
# autocrlf=true system-wide by default, which is why this repo stores LF - but that
# is a property of the machine that has always published it, not of the project.
# Anyone cloning on Linux, or on a Windows box with autocrlf off, gets different
# behaviour from the same repo.
#
# Adding this file changed nothing: every rule below matches what is already stored.

# Let git classify text vs binary per file, and store text files with LF. Checkout
# still honours core.autocrlf, so working copies stay CRLF on Windows.
* text=auto

# Archives. Auto-detection already spots these, but say it outright.
*.7z binary

# Copied byte-for-byte out of the build tree by sync_docs_files (normalize_lf=False),
# CRLF and all, because they are published as exact copies of the build inputs.
# Leave them alone in both directions.
Docs/PGO/background.js -text
Docs/PGO/manifest.json -text
Docs/PGO/pgo_done.html -text

# Patches/*.patch is deliberately NOT listed here, and that is a known compromise
# rather than an endorsement of the current behaviour.
#
# git format-patch emits mixed endings: LF for the patch's own structure, and the
# source file's real endings on the context and +/- content lines. Patch 0017
# deletes tools/upx-after-package.ps1, which is CRLF in the Firefox tree, so its
# removed-content lines are CRLF while its headers are LF. The text=auto rule above
# strips those CRs on commit, so the published patch describes an LF file where the
# real one is CRLF - which matters because Patches/README.md tells people to run
# `git am Patches/*.patch`.
#
# Correcting it means "*.patch -text" AND re-exporting all 17 patches, which
# rewrites published files. That is a deliberate change to make on its own, not a
# side effect of adding this file.
